// Register D24: the monomorphisation worklist's TRANSITIVE CLOSURE, and specifically
// the ORDER it emits in. `examples/lang/generic_calls_generic.sentinel` pins that the
// closure happens at all; this file pins that it happens in the RIGHT SEQUENCE, which
// is a separate property and the one that is easy to get wrong.
//
// The shape is a BRANCHING transitive graph — `main` calls two generics, each of which
// calls a different generic — because a single chain cannot distinguish the candidate
// implementations. On a chain they all agree; here they do not:
//
//   oracle / correct : g1__i64, g2__bool, h2__bool, h1__i64
//   naive LIFO drain : g2__bool, h2__bool, g1__i64, h1__i64
//   FIFO over a growing list : g1__i64, g2__bool, h1__i64, h2__bool
//
// ⚠ THE REGISTER'S PRESCRIPTION FOR D24 WAS "turn the loop into a LIFO worklist", AND
// THAT IS THE MIDDLE ROW — wrong, and wrong in a way the deferred program cannot show.
// The oracle keeps TWO structures: `order`, appended when an instance is first
// DISCOVERED and emitted from; and `pending`, a LIFO stack that only decides which body
// is re-walked next. Emission follows discovery, NOT the drain. A single emitting pass
// cannot reproduce it, because `h1` is discovered only after `h2` would already have
// been emitted — which is why `scg` now discovers under a muted pass and emits in a
// second one.
//
// ⚠ The two branches are kept at DIFFERENT type arguments (i64 and bool) on purpose. If
// both instantiated at the same type, the two halves would dedup against each other and
// the file would pin neither — the same trap `generic_calls_generic`'s own header warns
// about, and one an earlier draft of that file fell into.
//
// The exit code is the WEAK assertion here: it is identical under all three orderings.
// The real pin is the codegen differential, which sweeps `tests/pass` and compares the
// emitted IR byte-for-byte. If this file ever fails there, read the define order first.
//
// ⚠ THE SECOND HALF (`g`/`k1`/`k2`) EXISTS BECAUSE THE FIRST HALF IS ONE SHAPE SHORT.
// In `g1`/`g2` each body discovers exactly ONE nested instance, so the order in which a
// body's discoveries are pushed onto the pending stack is unobservable — a mutation
// that pushes them in REVERSE survives the first half untouched. `g` calls BOTH `h1`
// and `h2`, whose children are `k1` and `k2`, and the oracle emits
// `g, h1, h2, k2, k1`: `k2` before `k1` is the forward-push signature, and reversing
// the push flips exactly those two. The review found this gap; the first draft of this
// file had only the first half and pinned three of the four orderings.
//
// Trace: h1 20 + h2 22 = 42 from the first half; the second half's `gsum` is asserted
// to 0 so it cannot mask a wrong answer in the first. Exit 42, no stdout.

fn h1<T>(x: T) -> i64 {
    20
}

fn h2<T>(x: T) -> i64 {
    22
}

fn g1<T>(x: T) -> i64 {
    h1(x)
}

fn g2<T>(x: T) -> i64 {
    h2(x)
}

fn k1<T>(x: T) -> i64 {
    0
}

fn k2<T>(x: T) -> i64 {
    0
}

// Two nested discoveries in ONE body — this is what makes the child-push ORDER
// observable. `hh1`/`hh2` sit at different type arguments so they cannot dedup.
fn hh1<T>(x: T) -> i64 {
    k1(x)
}

fn hh2<T>(x: T) -> i64 {
    k2(x)
}

fn g<T>(x: T) -> i64 {
    hh1(x) + hh2(true)
}

fn main() -> i64 {
    let a: i64 = g1(1);
    let b: i64 = g2(true);
    let gsum: i64 = g(1);
    a + b + gsum
}
